WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.700
Nginx.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.705
Before a request reaches your Python code,

3
00:00:04.705 --> 00:00:08.698
something sits in front and handles the boring, critical jobs.

4
00:00:08.698 --> 00:00:09.600
That is Nginx.

5
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.448
Nginx is a reverse proxy.

6
00:00:12.448 --> 00:00:16.800
It receives every client request and forwards it to your backends.

7
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:24.333
At that choke point it ends HTTPS, so backends speak plain HTTP internally, and it balances load.

8
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:30.233
It also serves static files, and passes the real client address along in a forwarded header.

9
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:32.500
Here is the path.

10
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.433
The client sends an HTTPS request.

11
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:37.900
Nginx terminates TLS right here.

12
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.550
Then it fans requests out across identical replicas.

13
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:45.867
Whatever fancier gateway a company runs, the concepts are the same.

14
00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:49.400
Nginx has two strategies you should know.

15
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.067
Round-robin is the default: each replica takes a turn.

16
00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:55.563
It works when requests are similar.

17
00:00:55.563 --> 00:00:59.267
But when durations vary wildly, one replica can get buried.

18
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:04.400
Least connections sends work to the least busy replica, which suits LLM calls.

19
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.633
So for LLM traffic, choose least connections.

20
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:09.900
Now the config.

21
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.100
An upstream block lists the two replicas and picks least connections.

22
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.987
The server block listens on four four three with TLS and its certificate,

23
00:01:18.987 --> 00:01:21.500
then proxies every request to that pool.

24
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.097
And this last line matters for streaming.

25
00:01:24.097 --> 00:01:25.833
Remember it for the next scene.

26
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:29.000
Here is a classic interview story.

27
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.167
You put Nginx in front of two replicas, and load balancing works.

28
00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:37.300
But suddenly streaming delivers the whole answer in one lump at the end.

29
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.266
The culprit is the default proxy buffering.

30
00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.491
One directive, buffering off, and tokens flow again.

31
00:01:43.491 --> 00:01:46.033
The proxy is part of your streaming path.

32
00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:48.094
Let's recap.

33
00:01:48.094 --> 00:01:51.033
Nginx is the front door, a reverse proxy.

34
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:53.600
It ends TLS and balances load.

35
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.233
Least connections suits slow, uneven LLM calls.

36
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.950
And turn proxy buffering off for streams.

37
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.200
Next up: CI CD.
